    "body": "Laguna Verde lithium project. ( Image courtesy of Clean Tech Lithium. )\nBy: Staff Writer November 16, 2026\nCleanTech Lithium (AIM: CTL) faces another delay in obtaining a lithium operating contract for its Laguna Verde project in Chile after the country’s Comptroller General returned the approval decree to the government with observations, the company said Monday.\nThe regulator sent the decree back to the Ministry of Economy and Mining on Nov. 13, CleanTech said. Although it has received no official communication, the company understands the decree has not been rejected and the ministry is working to address the observations. It provided no revised approval timetable.\n“We are encouraged by the continued engagement and efforts of the Chilean authorities to progress the approval, even over this weekend,” CEO Ignacio Mehech said in the release.\nThe delay leaves the operating contract unresolved for a proposed $748-million (C$1.07-billion) development designed to produce 15,000 tonnes of battery-grade lithium carbonate annually over 25 years. CleanTech plans to use direct lithium extraction at Laguna Verde, about 275 km by road from Copiapó in northern Chile.\nCleanTech shares closed 5.3% stronger at 6p in London on Monday, valuing the company at about £21.9 million. The gain followed a 19% decline on Friday.\nFormer government\nThe company agreed contract terms with Chilean authorities in March, under former president Gabriel Boric’s administration, and initially expected ratification in the second quarter. The proposed 40-year Special Lithium Operating Contract, known by its Spanish acronym CEOL, covers 153 sq. km and would span exploration, evaluation, construction, production and closure.\nCleanTech has not disclosed the nature of the regulator’s observations. The contract’s ratification would not replace the project’s other permitting, technical work and financing requirements.\nA prefeasibility study released in March, with a correction issued in April, estimated the capital cost including contingency and outlined probable reserves of 378,000 tonnes of lithium carbonate equivalent at an average lithium concentration of 186 mg per litre.\nAn estimate issued in November 2025 put measured and indicated resources at 835,000 tonnes of lithium carbonate equivalent at 178 mg lithium per litre, plus inferred resources of 1.065 million tonnes at 167 mg per litre.\nThat resource estimate includes a surface-lake component, while the proposed operating contract excludes the lake surface. CleanTech said in March that the estimate might require revision to reflect the final contract boundary.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: lithium\nREGION: South America\nCOMPANY: CleanTech Lithium\nRepublish this article",
